KZ2 patch 6102 (2026-11-25): what the walker's readback fence waits wait for. Patch
6091's rbwait bucket (3.1-3.6 ms per flip in a gameplay test run) is the fence wait of every ld_flush(LD_FLUSH_READBACK), whatever asked
for it. Callers now name their site in g_ld_rb_site (walker thread; the outermost site wins): nv0039 (the NV0039
copy's source writeback, cellGcmSys.c), label (before_guest_sync from the 0x1D70/0x1D74 semaphore release in
rsx_commands.c and the 0x6C label release in cellGcmSys.c), present (before_guest_sync at present), gv (guest-visible
rows uploaded back, ld_gv_upload_rows), zp (z-pass readback, ld_zp_read_snapshot), other (the rest: dumps, debug
reads). With RSX_PERF_SPLIT=1 each wait's QPC time and count go to its site and one line follows each [perf-split]:
`[perf-split-rb] t=Ns other=MS/N nv0039=MS/N label=MS/N present=MS/N gv=MS/N zp=MS/N (ms/waits per flip)`.
Off without RSX_PERF_SPLIT (the site stores are two plain int writes per call). Measurement only, no behaviour change.
Not host-tested: the counting sits in the D3D12 flush path; checked by the build and the split run's line.
